.TH XD\-FLATPAK 1 2026-10-18 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-flatpak \- Flatpak application permissions and remotes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-flatpak
.OP \-\-page n
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-flatpak
(Flatpak in the Control Panel's Add or Remove Programs category) shows
what Flatpak applications may use outside their sandbox, lets the user
change it, and manages the remotes Flatpak installs from.
It works through libflatpak on the user's and the system's
installations.
.TP
.B Applications
The installed applications.
.B Permissions...
opens an application's permissions as they come from its metadata, its
runtime's and the overrides (the system's global and per-application
overrides for an application of a system installation, then the user's
global ones), with the user's own changes on top, shown in bold:
shared subsystems (network, IPC), sockets, devices and features on the
.B General
and
.B Devices
tabs; filesystems with their access (read and write, read only, created
if missing, no access) and persistent folders on
.BR "File System" ;
environment variables set or unset on
.BR Environment ;
and session and system bus names the application may see, talk to or own
on
.BR D\-Bus .
A change that matches what the application has anyway leaves no entry.
.B Restore Defaults
in the dialog, and
.B Reset
in the list, remove the user's changes.
Changes take effect the next time the application starts.
.TP
.B Remotes
The remotes of each installation: name, title, address, the
installation, and whether it's enabled.
.BR Add... ,
from a
.I .flatpakrepo
file (with the remote's signing key) or an address (without one),
.BR Remove ,
and
.BR Enable / Disable .
Changes to a system installation go through Flatpak's system helper,
which asks polkit for an administrator's password.
.PP
Portal permissions (screenshots, location and the like, kept by
xdg\-desktop\-portal's permission store) aren't shown here.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " n"
Open on the
.IR n th
tab, from 0 (Applications).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/flatpak/overrides/APP\-ID
The user's override for an application, in the format
.BR flatpak\-metadata (5)
describes and
.B flatpak override \-\-user
writes.
.B $FLATPAK_USER_DIR
moves the user installation, overrides included, as for
.BR flatpak (1).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/flatpak/overrides/global
.TQ
.I /var/lib/flatpak/overrides/
The user's overrides for all applications, and the system's, which are
read but not changed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR flatpak\-override (1),
.BR flatpak\-metadata (5),
.BR flatpak\-remote\-add (1),
.BR xd\-appwiz (1),
.BR xd\-control (1)
